.\" Manpage for clone.
.TH man 1 "30 September 2024" "1.0" "clone man page"
.SH NAME
clone.sh \- clone one drive to another using \fBrsync\fR(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clone.sh\fR \fI[\fB-e\fI exclude_file] [\fB-f\fI fstypes_file] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Source and destination drives need not be the same size as long as all source data fits on destination. If the source drive is bootable the destination will become bootable provided certain conditions are met (see \fBLIMITS\fR).

If \fBclone.sh\fR is run without any command line options the configuration files used are the defaults under \fI/etc/clone/\fR which can be overriden under \fI~/.config/clone/\fR.

After \fBclone.sh\fR is run a list of files/dirs excluded from cloning is displayed, e.g. system files under \fI/dev/*\fR, \fI/proc/*\fR, \fI/sys/*\fR, etc. These files/dirs are read from the configuration file \fIexclude\fR (see \fBFILES\fR). Also, a list of drives connected to the system is displayed and user input is required for source and destination drives. 

During cloning, appropriate messages are produced on standard output indicating which command is being executed and a progress indicator, including total bytes and % cloned, is displayed as well. Log files are produced under \fI/var/log/clone.sh_cdb68a8f-2050-4fcd-a789-c130cc98c1e5/\fR.

Multiple \fBclone.sh\fR instances can run in parallel provided the destination drives are (obviously) different. For example, if three drives are connected to the system one drive can be cloned to the other two at the same time.

While \fBclone.sh\fR is running it can be canceled with Ctrl-C.
.SH EXAMPLES
After \fBclone.sh\fR is run a user-friendly menu is displayed with the drives connected to the system prefixed by number. The user just inputs the source and destination numbers separated by a space, e.g. "1 2".
.SH FILES
The configuration files used by \fBclone.sh\fR are:

\fI/etc/clone/exclude\fR (see \fBman \fIclone-exclude\fR)

\fI/etc/clone/fstypes\fR (contains commands to format a partition for various filesystems)

The user can create custom files under \fI~/.config/clone/\fR but the default filenames have to be used. It is recommended not to modify the default \fIfstypes\fR file or create your own unless you really know what you are doing.

\fBclone.sh\fR searches for custom files if not run under its installation directory (usually \fI/usr/sbin/clone/\fR). The directory of the custom files must be \fI~/.config/clone/\fR and the default filenames have to be used.

Custom files can also be provided when executing \fBclone.sh\fR with the \fB-e\fR or \fB-f\fR command line options (see \fIOPTIONS\fR). This takes precedence over any other files and any valid pathnames can be used.

Every time a cloning session is executed log directories from previous runs are deleted and the directory \fI/var/log/clone.sh_cdb68a8f-2050-4fcd-a789-c130cc98c1e5/<x>_<y>/\fR is created where \fI<x>\fR is the number of the source drive and \fI<y>\fR is the number of the destination drive. The following files are created under this directory:

\fIlog\fR      (contains files/dirs cloned)

\fIerrors\fR   (contains any errors encountered)

\fIcommands\fR (contains most commands executed by \fBclone.sh\fR)

Sometimes it is useful to \fBtail\fR(1) the \fIlog\fR file to see which files/dirs are being transferred by \fBrsync\fR(1).

Before cloning begins the following lock directory and files under it are created:

\fI/var/lock/clone.sh_cdb68a8f-2050-4fcd-a789-c130cc98c1e5/\fR

    \fIclone_pids\fR: ensures that \fBclone.sh\fR is executed only if its source and destination are not destinations of another \fBclone.sh\fR instance

    \fIslp_clone_pid\fR: ensures that only one \fBclone.sh\fR instance is responsible for disabling/enabling sleep (suspend/hibernate)

The lock directory is deleted after cloning has completed.

If multiple clone sessions are executed in parallel a log directory per session is created.
.SH OPTIONS
\fB-e\fR \fIexclude_file\fR

\fB-f\fR \fIfstypes_file\fR

If any options are provided they supercede the default configuration files (see \fBFILES\fR). Must be valid pathnames.
.SH LIMITS
\fBclone.sh\fR must be run with root privileges.

\fBclone.sh\fR must be run on a system that uses \fBsystemd\fR(1) as init.

The source drive must not use Logical Volume Management (\fBlvm\fR(8)).

The source drive must have partitions with UUIDs.

If the source drive has partitions with the btrfs filesystem any subvolumes on them will not be created on the destination. To see which filesystems are fully supported check \fIfstypes\fR file (see \fBFILES\fR).

If the source drive is bootable the destination will become bootable only if the source drive and the system \fBclone.sh\fR is run on use GRUB 2 as their bootloader. Regardless, the destination will contain all source data provided cloning was successful.

.SH EXIT_STATUS
Zero on success else > 1. In case of error an appropriate error message is displayed and further info for the error might be available in the error file (see \fBFILES\fR).
.SH NOTES
To clone, \fBclone.sh\fR is using the \fBrsync\fR(1) \fI--inc-recursive\fR (\fI--i-r\fR) command line option in order to use less memory. However, this option interferes with the \fBrsync\fR(1) \fI--progress\fR command line option used in \fBclone.sh\fR. The percentage of files transferred that is displayed is incorrect but the data size is correct.
.SH CAVEATS
If many programs are running while \fBclone.sh\fR is running then it is possible that these programs will delete some of the files they use during cloning. However, the deleted files are part of \fBrsync\fR(1)'s list of files to transfer. In that case, when \fBrsync\fR(1) exits, its return code is 24 (partial transfer due to vanished source files) but \fBclone.sh\fR ignores it. To avoid this it is recommended that prior to executing \fBclone.sh\fR all programs on all user accounts are closed and all user accounts are logged out but one that has root privileges.
.SH BUGS
If sleep (suspend/hibernate) is enabled on the system \fBclone.sh\fR is run on then it will disable sleep during cloning. If \fBclone.sh\fR is run from a XFCE terminal window and it disables hibernation and the hibernation period expires during cloning then when cloning has completed \fBclone.sh\fR enables hibernation but XFCE can not hibernate unless the terminal window is closed. Insofar this has not been identified as a \fBclone.sh\fR bug.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBclone-exclude\fR(5), \fBrsync\fR(1), \fBsystemd\fR(1), \fBlvm\fR(8), \fBgrub-install\fR(8), \fBgrub-mkconfig\fR(8)
